{"content":"Perfect! Now I have all the evidence. Let me write the answer:\n\nh05.\n\n**Mechanism: Explicit Go code (no database-level rule)**\n\nWhen a user is deleted, emoji reactions are removed by **explicit Go code**, not by database-level foreign key constraints. The `reaction` table has no foreign key relationship to the `user` table.\n\n**Evidence locations:**\n\n1. **Reaction table schema** (no foreign keys):\n   - MySQL: `store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql` (lines 90–98) — defines `reaction` table with only a unique constraint on `(creator_id, content_id, reaction_type)`, no foreign key to `user` table\n   - PostgreSQL: `store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql` (lines 91–98) — identical structure, no foreign keys\n\n2. **Explicit deletion in Go code**:\n   - **MySQL implementation**: `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go` lines 96–104 in `deleteUserTargetsTx()`:\n     - Line 96: `deleteReactionsByContentIDsTx()` deletes reactions on all memos created by the user\n     - Line 102: `deleteReactionsByCreatorTx()` at line 437–439 deletes all reactions created by the user with SQL: `DELETE FROM reaction WHERE creator_id = ?`\n   - **PostgreSQL implementation**: `store/db/postgres/user_delete.go` lines 96–104 (same functions), with `deleteReactionsByCreatorTx()` at line 400–403\n   - **API layer**: `server/router/api/v1/user_service.go` lines 393–455 in `DeleteUser()` calls `s.Store.DeleteUser()` which triggers all the above cleanup\n\nThe code explicitly removes reactions both by content (when memos are deleted) and by creator (when the user who created the reaction is deleted).","iteration":21,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
